<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="cutin biosynthesis" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-321" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Ubiquitous Plant Pathways (UPP) that was generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are thought to be present in all or most land plants. All UPP pathways are automatically imported into databases regardless of Pathologic predictions. &lt;A href=&quot;https://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; The plant cuticle is a lipophilic layer that covers all above-ground epidermal surfaces of vascular plants and functions as a barrier to control the movement of gas, water and solutes, as well as protect from plant pathogens. The cuticles is composed of two main ingredients - |FRAME: Cutins cutin| and cuticular wax. |FRAME: Cutins Cutin| is a polymer comprised of |FRAME: OMEGA-HYDROXY-FATTY-ACID &quot;&amp;omega;-hydroxy fatty acids&quot;| and their derivatives, interlinked via ester bonds and forming a polyester polymer of indeterminate size. There are two major families of cutin monomers, comprising C16 and C18 compounds. The C16 family consists mainly of |FRAME: 16-HYDROXYPALMITATE| and |FRAME: CPD-17653| and |FRAME: 1016-DIHYDROXYPALMITATE|. The C18 family consists mainly of |FRAME: 18-HYDROXYOLEATE|, |FRAME: 910-EPOXY-18-HYDROXYSTEARATE|, and |FRAME: 91018-TRIHYDROXYSTEARATE|. Cutin biosynthesis consists of sequential reactions including the activation of acyl chains to acyl-CoAs by the long-chain acyl-CoA synthetase encoded by the |FRAME: AT1G49430| gene, hydroxylation and epoxidation catalyzed by several cytochrome P450 family members, and esterification to glycerol-3-phosphate by glycerol-3-phosphate acyltransferases (GPATs) |CITS: [25053648]| Among the enzymes characterized from this pathway, the Arabidopsis |FRAME: AT2G45970| and |FRAME: AT2G27690| are fatty acid &amp;omega;-hydroxylases that display distinct substrate specificities. CYP86A8 hydroxylates palmitate and stearate, but not expoxy fatty acids |CITS: [11493698]|, while CYP94C1 metabolize the latter more effectively |CITS: [17868380]|. A CYP P450 activity detected in soybean also had preference towards fatty acid epoxides |CITS: [Blee93]|. Note that even though &amp;alpha;,&amp;omega;-Dicarboxylic acids are signatures of suberin (see |FRAME: PWY-1121|) and are generally found only in trace level in cutin, they count for more than 50% of cutin monomers in Arabidopsis.</Description>
