    <DataNode elementId="MONOMER-17705" textLabel="long-chain fatty acid ω-hydroxylase" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="Q8S7V0" dataSource="uniprot" />
      <Graphics centerX="850.9545288085938" centerY="811.9066772460938" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="PMN, dreher">CYP704B2 is a p450 monoxygenase that is involved in cutin biosynthesis. Plants with mutations in CYP704B2 are male sterile with defects in pollen exine development and almost completely lack cutin monomers on their anthers |CITS: [20086189]|. Enzymatic assays with CYP704B2 expressed in yeast demonstrate that this protein catalyzes the &amp;omega;-hydroxylation of several fatty acids including |FRAME: PALMITATE palmitate|, |FRAME: OLEATE-CPD oleate|, |FRAME: LINOLEIC_ACID linoleate|, and |FRAME: LINOLENIC_ACID &amp;alpha;-linolenate| |CITS: [20086189]|. It did not show any activity against two other potential cutin monomer precursors |FRAME: DODECANOATE laurate| and |FRAME: CPD-10514 9,10-epoxystearate| in this assay. |CITS: [20086189]|. Further analyses using &lt;i&gt;cyp704B2&lt;/i&gt; plant extracts and the yeast-expressed enzyme demonstrated that |FRAME: PALMITATE palmitate| is most likely to be the endogenous substrate of this enzyme. Fatty acyl-CoAs could also act as substrates of this enzyme, but that activity could not be assayed in the yeast experimental set-up |CITS: [20086189]|. It was also impossible to determine whether this enzyme contributes to |FRAME: PWY-6733 sporopollenin precursors biosynthesis|, but it may play a role in that important process as well. CYP704B2 transcripts appear to be expressed in microspores and the tapetal cell layer of anthers, consistent with the &lt;i&gt;cyp704B2&lt;/i&gt; mutant phenotype and with the proposed role of this protein in anther cutin biosynthesis. Interestingly, putative orthologs of this gene in other plant species appear to be expressed in reproductive organs and might share similar biological and biochemical functions from bryophytes to flowering plants |CITS: [20086189]|.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="MONOMER-17705" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Pfam" value="PF00067" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Interpro" value="IPR001128" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Prints" value="PR00463" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Uniprot" value="Q8S7V0" />
      <Property key="Catalyzes" value="RXN-20934, RXN-20933" />
      <Property key="CatalyzesCount" value="2" />
      <Property key="Gene" value="G-15403" />
      <Property key="InstanceNameTemplate" value="MONOMER-*" />
      <CitationRef elementRef="citation_PUB_20086189" />
    </DataNode>
